course: books
lesson: 6
title: Alice in Wonderland, chapter III, part 2: Prizes, a long tale, and Dinah again
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Everybody has won the race, so everybody needs a prize, and the Dodo says Alice must give them; luckily she has a box of comfits (little sugar sweets) in her pocket, exactly one each.
explain: Then the Mouse says Alice needs a prize too, so the Dodo takes her own thimble and solemnly presents it back to her, and everyone cheers; Alice finds it absurd but keeps a straight face.
explain: The Mouse begins its "long and sad tale", but Alice is staring at its long tail, mixes up the words, and then hears "not" as "knot", so the Mouse stomps off insulted.
explain: Feeling lonely, Alice wishes her cat Dinah were there and proudly says Dinah eats little birds, and at once every bird finds an excuse to hurry home.
explain: Left alone, she cries again, until she hears little footsteps and hopes the Mouse is coming back: a funny part with a sad ending, about not always noticing how your words land.
fact: The Dodo points at Alice: "Why, she, of course" must give the prizes, and the party crowd round her shouting "Prizes! Prizes!"
fact: Alice pulls out a box of comfits (sugar-coated sweets) that the salt water had not got into, and there is "exactly one a-piece, all round"; "a-piece" means each.
fact: The Mouse says Alice must have a prize herself; all she has left is a thimble (a small metal cap that protects your finger when sewing).
fact: The Dodo presents the thimble with "We beg your acceptance of this elegant thimble", and they all cheer.
fact: Alice thinks it "very absurd" (silly), but they look so grave (serious) that she dares not laugh, so she just bows.
fact: Eating the comfits is noisy: the large birds cannot taste theirs, and the small ones choke and have to be patted on the back.
fact: Alice whispers "C and D" for cats and dogs, so as not to upset the Mouse.
fact: The Mouse says "Mine is a long and a sad tale!" and Alice answers "It is a long tail, certainly", mixing up tale (story) and tail.
fact: In the tale a dog called Fury says to a mouse, "I'll be judge, I'll be jury", which means one side would decide everything, a very unfair trial; a "cur" is a bad-tempered dog and "prosecute" means take someone to court.
fact: In printed copies of the book the tale winds down the page in the shape of a tail, getting smaller.
fact: "You are not attending!" (not paying attention) says the Mouse; Alice thinks it has got "to the fifth bend", and when it cries "I had not!", she thinks it said "a knot" and offers to undo it.
fact: An old Crab tells her daughter "never to lose your temper", and the daughter snaps "Hold your tongue, Ma! You're enough to try the patience of an oyster!"
fact: Alice says Dinah will "eat a little bird as soon as look at it", and the birds leave on "various pretexts" (made-up excuses).
fact: The old Magpie says "the night-air doesn't suit my throat", and a Canary calls its children to bed.
fact: Alice feels "very lonely and low-spirited" (sad) and cries, then hears pattering footsteps and hopes the Mouse has changed its mind.
fact: Old words in today's English: "grave" = serious, "solemnly" = very seriously, "venture to ask" = dare to ask, "you can't think" = you can't imagine.
exercise: Who did the Dodo say must give the prizes? | Alice | The Mouse | The Lory | Nobody at all
exercise: What did the party shout as they crowded round Alice? | "Prizes! Prizes!" | "Speak English, please!" | "Off with her head!" | "Hooray for the Dodo!"
exercise: What did Alice find in her pocket to use as prizes? | A box of comfits | A bag of smooth pebbles | Some little cakes | A pile of little keys
exercise: What are comfits? | Little sugar-coated sweets | Soft round cushions to sit on | Small silver coins | Tiny paper flags
exercise: Why were the comfits still good to eat? | The salt water had not got into the box | Alice had kept them high above her head | They were wrapped in the Rabbit's kid gloves | Comfits cannot be spoilt by water at all
exercise: How many comfits did each creature get? | Exactly one each | Two each | Half each | A whole handful each
exercise: Who said Alice must have a prize herself? | The Mouse | The old Dodo | The Eaglet | The little Duck
exercise: What else did Alice have in her pocket? | Only a thimble | A tiny golden key | A ball of string | Her sister's book
exercise: What is a thimble? | A small metal cap that protects your finger when sewing | A little bell that rings at the end of a race | A tiny cup that is used for drinking tea from at parties | A small brush for cleaning birds' feathers
exercise: What did the Dodo do with Alice's thimble? | Presented it back to her as her prize | Gave it to the Mouse as the winner | Kept it for itself to wear on its beak | Threw it into the pool of tears for luck
exercise: Why did Alice not laugh when given her own thimble? | They all looked so grave that she did not dare | She thought it was a wonderful prize indeed | She was too cold and wet to laugh at anything | She had a comfit stuck in her throat at the time
exercise: What trouble did the large birds have with the comfits? | They could not taste theirs | They choked and coughed | They dropped them in the mud | They wanted two each
exercise: What happened to the small birds eating comfits? | They choked and had to be patted on the back | They could not taste a thing and complained | They flew off home to save them for later on in the week | They fell asleep in the grass straight away
exercise: Why did Alice whisper "C and D"? | To avoid saying "cats" and "dogs" and offending the Mouse | Because those were the first letters of the Mouse's name | To spell out a secret code that only the Dodo knew | Because her throat was very sore from swallowing all the salty water
exercise: What did the Mouse say about its story? | "Mine is a long and a sad tale!" | "Mine is a short and a funny tale!" | "Mine is the driest thing I know!" | "Mine is a story about the Queen!"
exercise: What did Alice think the Mouse meant by "tale"? | Its long tail | A long fairy story | A ticket for the bus | A list of sums
exercise: In the Mouse's tale, what is the name of the dog? | Fury | Rover | Patch | Dinah
exercise: What does Fury say he will be at the trial? | Both judge and jury | Only the jury | The mouse's lawyer | A kind and honest witness
exercise: What did the Mouse say when Alice was not listening? | "You are not attending!" | "Speak English, if you please!" | "Ahem!" | "Ugh!"
exercise: What did Alice think the Mouse meant when it cried "I had not!"? | A knot that needed undoing | A nut that it had found | A note it had written | A gnat that had bitten it
exercise: What advice did the old Crab give her daughter? | Never to lose her temper | Always to go to bed early | Never to talk to strangers | Always to say please
exercise: What did the young Crab say her mother was enough to try? | The patience of an oyster | The temper of a mouse in a hurry | The kindness of a dodo | The wisdom of a lory
exercise: What did Alice say Dinah does with little birds? | Eats one "as soon as look at it" | Plays with them in the garden | Sings with them every morning | Chases them off the roof
exercise: What excuse did the Magpie give for leaving? | The night-air did not suit its throat | It had left its eggs alone in the nest | It was already late for tea with the Duchess | It did not like the sugary taste of comfits
exercise: Here "pretexts" means... | Made-up excuses | Old letters | Long stories told at night | Small prizes
exercise: How did Alice feel when everyone had gone? | Very lonely and low-spirited | Proud and very pleased with herself | Glad to have some peace and quiet | Sleepy and ready for her bed
exercise: Thinking back: when else had Alice talked about Dinah and upset an animal? | In the pool, when she told the Mouse about her | In the hall, when she talked to the Rabbit | On the bank, when she talked to her sister | In the well, when she met a little bat
exercise: Thinking back: where did Alice last sit alone crying before this? | In the hall, after the key and the door went wrong | In the garden, after the Rabbit had shouted at her | On the bank, after her sister had read to her | In the well, after she had dropped the marmalade jar
test: Ellie goes: "The Dodo gave Alice a gold thimble from its own pocket." Put her right. | It was Alice's own thimble, given back to her | It was the Mouse's thimble, lent to Alice | It was a thimble the Rabbit had dropped | It was a sugar comfit shaped just like a thimble
test: Kai, Gen Alpha: "Getting your own thimble as a prize? That's so skibidi." ("skibidi" = a word kids shout to mean weird or silly.) What did Alice think of it? | She thought it very absurd but kept a straight face | She thought it was the best prize she had ever won | She was cross and refused to take the thimble back | She laughed so much that the Dodo was offended
test: Why is "It is a long tail, certainly" funny? | "Tale" and "tail" sound the same but mean different things | Mice really do tell their stories by waving their tails about | Alice had never seen a mouse's tail before | The Mouse had a very short tail indeed
test: What would have happened if Alice had not had the box of comfits? | There would have been no prizes for anyone | The Dodo would have given out its feathers | The Mouse would have won the race instead | The race would have started again at once
test: Grandma, Kentucky: "Bless her heart, that child cain't keep her mouth shut about that cat." ("bless her heart" = a kind way to say someone's being silly.) What happens when Alice talks about Dinah this time? | All the birds make excuses and leave | The Mouse comes back and joins in | The Lory asks to meet Dinah at once | The Dodo awards Dinah a prize
test: True or false: the Mouse leaves because Alice talked about cats again. | False: it leaves because she wasn't listening and said "a knot" | True: she whispered "C and D" much too loudly | True: she said Dinah was a capital mouse-catcher | False: it leaves because the race had started again
test: Danny, rough: "Hold your tongue, Ma!" Who says this, and to whom? | The young Crab, to her mother | Alice, to her sister | The Lory, to the Dodo | The Mouse, to Alice
test: Why is it funny that the young Crab snaps at her mother right after the "never lose your temper" advice? | She loses her temper at once | She agrees and says thank you | She runs off to find the Mouse | She asks the Mouse to come back
test: Why does the Mouse's tale show that Fury's trial is unfair? | Fury wants to be judge and jury himself | The mouse has a very clever lawyer | The trial is held in the garden | There are far too many people sitting on the jury
test: Ifeoma from Lagos: "The birds no wan stay at all, dem just japa." ("japa" = run away, leave fast.) Why did they "japa"? | Alice said Dinah eats little birds | It had started to rain very hard | The Dodo said the party was over | The comfits had all been eaten up
test: Looking back to chapter II: what did the Mouse promise on the shore, and did it keep the promise? | To tell its history; it began but walked off | To show Alice the garden; it did so at once | To dry everyone; it did so with its history | To find the Rabbit; it never even began
test: Looking back: Alice wanted the Mouse to come back in the pool too. What did she say then to make it return? | That they would not talk about cats or dogs | That she would give it a box of comfits | That she would take it to the lovely garden | That she would let it ride on her shoulder
test: How is Alice the same now as at the end of chapter II's first part, alone in the hall? | She is alone again, crying and hoping someone will come | She is nine feet tall again and stuck in a room | She is again trying to say a poem and getting it wrong | She is again falling slowly down a very deep well
test: Looking back: Alice once heard "a little pattering of feet" in the hall. Who was it then? | The White Rabbit | The Mouse | The Dodo | Dinah the cat, come down the hole
test: ★★★ At the start of the prize-giving Alice had a box of comfits and a thimble. What did she have at the end of it? | Just her own thimble | Nothing at all | A thimble and a comfit | A new box of comfits
test: ★★★ Alice thought the Mouse had got "to the fifth bend" of its tale. What was she really looking at? | The bends in its long tail | The pages of a book | The turns of the race-course | The bends of the river
test: ★★★ "I had not!" and "A knot!" sound the same. Which pair below also sound the same but mean different things, like tale and tail? | Night and knight | Cat and dog | Long and short | Race and prize
exam: Identify the article distributed by the protagonist to the company as prizes. | Comfits | Thimbles | Pebbles | Cakes
exam: Ascertain the provenance of the elegant thimble presented to the protagonist by the Dodo. | It had previously been the protagonist's own property | It had been obtained from the residence of the Rabbit | It had been the personal property of the Dodo | It had been recovered from the pool of tears
exam: It is given that the Mouse exclaimed "I had not!". Determine the interpretation erroneously placed upon this utterance by the protagonist. | That the Mouse referred to a knot requiring to be undone | That the Mouse denied having commenced its narrative | That the Mouse refused to accept a comfit as a prize | That the Mouse required the presence of the Dodo
exam: Select the response most consistent with the established facts concerning the departure of the birds. | It followed the protagonist's account of Dinah's habits | It followed the conclusion of the Caucus-race prizes | It followed a summons issued by the White Rabbit | It followed an interruption by the elderly Crab
